[Music]  tens of thousands of brazilians  demonstrated against the government last  weekend they're frustrated with the  handling of the pandemic so far nearly  600 000 brazilians have died from the  virus it's the highest death toll in the  world after the united states  many brazilians lost their jobs during  lockdowns while prices for rent food and  gas are on the rise  much of the south american giants  population has been left impoverished  with no way out in sight  hello and welcome to our copy 19 special  i'm monica jones good to have you with  us  well once projected to be the fifth  largest economy in the world brazil has  tumbled to number 12.  in the country's largest city sao paulo  entire families are now homeless because  they can no longer afford to pay the  rent the coronavirus pandemic hitting an  already fragile economy for many  brazilians it's the perfect storm  tresina used to live alone near downtown  sao paulo but when she could no longer  afford the rent she moved here on the  outskirts of the city where she now  lives with a niece  her daily battle is to buy food  this is the cheapest place to market in  her neighborhood yet she has to  negotiate prices the economic crisis  caused by the pandemic almost destroyed  her job as a freelance pollster  she's 61 years old has no husband or  children and is now running out of money  today i leave the market with my card  half full  before with the same money i would live  with a full cart and even with little  bugs in my hands  trezinha is just one example of what  millions of brazilians are experiencing  in these days according to the latest  authorities report just in the month of  july in the state of sao paulo there was  an 11 increase in food prices  the economic crisis is visible in the  streets of sao paulo a hundred people  live here in prasadese in the herd of  the city  here they sleep  cook  and eat  although there are no official figures  on the increase of homelessness in the  city ngos agree that it has skyrocket  surface foundation provides meals for  the homeless on a daily basis before the  pandemic they serve about 12 000 meals a  month in august 2021 the number doubled  now in 2021 many families arrive  women women with children  the cost of living in sao paulo has  risen dramatically over the last few  months  the institute of economic research  determined that electricity for example  increased 25 percent during the last  year  other items such as meat have registered  increases of up to 40 percent  we have inflation which destroys  purchasing power and on the other the  difficulties in the labor markets are  consequence of the pandemic in addition  politicians are not offering a solution  for families it is a critical moment  terezinha notes the rising of cost of  living her electricity bill for august  was 30 percent more expensive than the  previous month  debts keep her awake at nights  and the future for brazilians is not  bright according to official figures 14  of the population is unemployed as the  country reports the highest inflation  rates of the last 20 years and the  pandemic which caused the crisis shows  no signs of coming to an end  for more i'm joined by lina simmet  senior researcher on poverty and  inequality at human rights watch and she  joins us in new york good morning to you  i  just talked about a perfect storm and  normally we use that phrase when two  critical events come together in  brazil's case it's certainly the  coronavirus pandemic and what else  in fact there are many things that are  coming together in brazil but one of  them is certainly this huge inequality  crisis  um that has been boiling in in brazil  for for many years brazil is one of the  most unequal countries in the world and  the most unequal country in the region  and we see how the pandemic has really  exacerbated many of these existing  economic but also social inequalities in  terms of who has been pushed into  poverty who cannot afford food or water  um  the way they need to and now rely on um  sort of unsafe systems  um or the transfers that the government  has not provided to them sufficiently  where else in the world do we see  similar patterns especially now during  the pandemic  which is clearly driving poverty  in fact we see it all over the world see  the the covert pandemic has exacerbated  inequality  um in in european countries  in latin america as as much as in in  asia or or africa  and what we see is that in countries  that already had high inequality  um and lacking social protection systems  that's really where sort of the the  poverty crisis now  is among the worst because these these  systems really lack protection for  people who lost income lost jobs during  the pandemic and who have not recovered  and many of these workers  or many of these people work in the  informal economy and and just to say  that this is not a small share of the  population but in in many regions it's  the vast majority of the people working  informally without labor protections  without social protections when they  lose work when they lose income when  they fall ill  and just means having no income at all  and no ability to buy  um the things you need for for basic  survival  but clearly when a huge part of the  population suffers uh as you just  described in your research certainly  focuses on social protection measures  that fail to prevent people from falling  into poverty uh a lot has went has gone  wrong what can be done better uh next  time  i would say at the beginning of the  pandemic it was actually quite  encouraging to see how countries have  expanded existing protection measures or  introduced new ones  but  many of the flaws that we that we have  seen so clearly this last year those are  the ones that should be fixed moving  forward and that includes inadequate  coverage um where in the case of nigeria  just to give you an example  less than one percent of the population  received cash transfers  last year although more than 50 of the  population went hungry  um  in the amounts of social protection were  also often insufficient with  monthly payments of less than the  minimum wage or  or less than the the poverty threshold  in the respective countries  we  um  we also see that in  that in many instances there were issues  around corruption so the lack of  transparency where the money has gone  um and and therefore really relying sort  of on on informal systems since the  countries haven't set up sort of solid  structures for social protection systems  prior so really rethinking the need for  um investing in stronger systems moving  forward is absolutely essential right i  mean investing in stronger system there  are some who argue that we actually need  a global health governance rooted in  principles of justice and governed by  the common good and uh an institution to  represent and serve these common  interests as well as a moral  constitution for our planet's health  that's quite a tall order and i take it  the who does not fit the bill here what  exactly are we looking at  so  i would say in in two areas on the one  hand  around the global structures i think on  on vaccinations at least we need to  really push  powerful and higher income countries in  sharing the recipe of the vaccine with  others because the the inequalities  currently in vaccination coverage and  access  are enormous nowhere in high-income  countries you have 60  percent of the population  now vaccinated and in poor countries  it's just three percent right so really  much of the second quality  and the second one relates to social  protection here too um higher income  countries had a much greater possibility  to to invest and and protect their um  their populations and therefore there  should be more solidarity in building  out um  some sort of global fund for for social  protection all right lina seaman there  thank you so much for your time  thank you  time for one of your questions now  here's derek williams  are there any studies showing that air  pollution increases mortality from covet  19  long-term exposure to significant levels  of air pollution is a major factor in  death  and suffering worldwide the who  estimates that it kills around  seven million people a year globally  that's more than the number of deaths  caused by infectious diseases and and  similar to the number associated with  smoking so  it's no wonder that many studies have  looked at the links between air  pollution and covet 19 during the course  of the pandemic  and it shouldn't come as much of a  surprise that a potentially serious  respiratory disease  can pretty clearly affect people a lot  worse who are regularly exposed to high  levels of air contaminants  let's look a little closer at just one  of those studies which searched for  correlations  between air pollution and covid cases in  england last summer  it found even after adjusting for  confounding factors like like population  density and age and income  that in areas with regular high levels  of toxic gases and particulate matter  uh places like london or the midlands  region  residents were significantly more likely  to be infected with saris kobe 2 or to  die from covet 19  than people in other parts of the  country the association was  particularly strong when it came to  areas with high levels of the nitrogen  oxides and and very fine particulate  matter  that mostly comes from burning fuel  the researchers concluded that even a  small increase in air pollution had a  big effect on both covet infectivity  and mortality in the country and and  there are plenty of other studies from  other countries that back up  and corroborate those results  and that's all for this edition of our  copied 19 special from me and the team  thanks for watching  [Music]  you

